---
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
import DirectionDemo from "../../components/DirectionDemo";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
---

<Docs
  title="Direction"
  description="Tells Sprõut's Base UI parts whether text reads left to right or right to left."
>
  <h1 class="docs-title">Direction</h1>
  <p class="docs-lead">
    Tells Sprõut's Base UI parts whether text reads left to right or right to
    left, so arrow keys, indicators, and popups follow the language on the page.
  </p>
  <div class="mt-8"><DirectionDemo client:load /></div>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Usage</h2>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="tsx"
      title="Direction usage"
      code={`import { DirectionProvider } from "@tulipfarm/sprout/direction";

<DirectionProvider direction="rtl">
  <div dir="rtl">
    <Breadcrumb>
      <BreadcrumbList>
        <BreadcrumbItem>
          <BreadcrumbLink href="/projects">المشاريع</BreadcrumbLink>
        </BreadcrumbItem>
        <BreadcrumbSeparator />
        <BreadcrumbItem>
          <BreadcrumbPage>الحديقة</BreadcrumbPage>
        </BreadcrumbItem>
      </BreadcrumbList>
    </Breadcrumb>
  </div>
</DirectionProvider>`}
    />
    <p>
      <code>direction</code> is <code>"ltr"</code> (the default) or
      <code>"rtl"</code>. The provider renders no element and does not set the
      <code>dir</code> attribute. Set a matching <code>dir</code> on a wrapping element,
      or on <code>html</code> for a whole app, so text, layout, and CSS such as the
      breadcrumb chevron follow the same direction.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Reading the direction</h2>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="tsx"
      title="useDirection"
      code={`import { useDirection } from "@tulipfarm/sprout/direction";

function NextStepIcon() {
  const direction = useDirection();
  return direction === "rtl" ? <ArrowLeft /> : <ArrowRight />;
}`}
    />
    <p>
      <code>useDirection()</code> returns the nearest provider's direction, or
      <code>"ltr"</code> outside one. Use it in your own parts that pick icons or
      keys by direction.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Accessibility</h2>
    <p>
      Arrow keys in toggle groups, tabs, menus, and other composite parts follow
      the provider: in right-to-left text the left arrow moves to the next item.
      Focus rings and transitions are those of each part. The provider has no
      styles or theme tokens of its own.
    </p>
  </section>
</Docs>
